Pityriasis Versicolor
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Dyspigmented macules/patches, Upper truncal distribution, Fine branny scaling

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-032
Reason for Visit: Sudden exacerbation of widespread, disfiguring truncal pigmentation causing severe psychological distress prior to a significant social event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult in acute psychosocial distress due to a sudden, highly visible, widespread skin discoloration that had rapidly worsened after returning from a tropical holiday. The patient was severely anxious about a potential spreading skin disease and completely unable to sleep due to anxiety. Urgent assessment was necessary to provide a definitive benign diagnosis, rule out an infectious or autoimmune process, and initiate immediate topical antifungal therapy to begin clearance.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest and back are covered in these terrible white and brown splotches. They seemed to multiply in the sun, and I look diseased. I have a wedding this weekend."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the presence of multiple asymptomatic or mildly pruritic well-demarcated macules and thin plaques. The lesions are appearing hypopigmented against the patient's surrounding tanned skin. The predominant distribution is on the upper trunk, neck, and proximal upper extremities. The patient notes a dramatic exacerbation following a recent tropical holiday characterized by hot, humid weather and excessive sweating. They have observed the failure of the hypopigmented lesions to tan with sun exposure, making them highly prominent. The coalescence of smaller macules into larger confluent patches across the chest has triggered extreme distress regarding their physical appearance.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute psychosocial distress and body image concerns.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but highly anxious and distressed when exposing the trunk for examination.
Physical Exam: The anterior chest, upper back, and proximal shoulders display widespread, well-demarcated hypopigmented macules and patches. There are also scattered mildly erythematous macules. A critical finding is elicited upon stretching or lightly scraping the skin: a fine, branny (dust-like) scaling is clearly visible on the surface of the lesions. The surrounding unaffected skin is heavily tanned. There is a complete lack of significant mucosal or appendageal involvement. No induration, vesicles, or excoriations are present.
Environmental Observations: Clean home.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pityriasis (Tinea) Versicolor - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Vitiligo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of fine branny scaling upon stretching the skin (vitiligo is completely smooth and devoid of scale) and the specific upper truncal distribution rather than classic acral/periorificial sites.

Pityriasis Rosea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, dyspigmented nature of the macules, lack of a herald patch, and absence of the classic collarette of scale and Christmas tree distribution.

Secondary Syphilis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of palmar/plantar involvement, absence of generalized lymphadenopathy, and the distinct presence of fine superficial scaling.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (benign condition, high anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly distressing exacerbation of Pityriasis Versicolor, classic in its upper truncal distribution, dyspigmentation, and fine branny scale following tropical sun exposure. Triage correctly identified the severe psychosocial distress and panic regarding a potentially disfiguring disease as an acute crisis for this patient. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was vital to physically rule out vitiligo or secondary syphilis, and to definitively diagnose the condition as a benign, superficial fungal overgrowth. Providing immediate clinical reassurance and prescribing targeted topical antifungal treatments successfully de-escalated the acute psychological distress and allowed the patient to sleep.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ketoconazole 2% shampoo to be used as a body wash (applied to wet skin, left for 10 minutes, then rinsed off) daily for 5-7 days. Provided extensive reassurance regarding the completely benign and non-contagious nature of the yeast overgrowth.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying the medicated shampoo over your entire trunk and leaving it on for a full 10 minutes before rinsing, you will effectively kill the yeast causing the rash, stopping it from spreading any further. By avoiding heavy, occlusive clothing and showering immediately after sweating, you will remove the hot, humid environment that this natural skin yeast needs to overgrow, preventing future flare-ups.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to ensure clearance of the active scaling.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the rash begins to blister, if the skin becomes hot and painful, or if you develop a fever, present to a medical centre or Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The condition is chronic and highly recurrent, especially in hot, humid climates.
Clinical Course: The active scaling and any mild itching will resolve within a week of using the shampoo. However, the white (hypopigmented) spots will remain for several months until the skin naturally sheds and repigments. The yeast is dead, but the "bleaching" effect takes time to reverse.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Ensure you treat the entire trunk, neck, and arms down to the elbows, even areas that look normal, to prevent immediate recurrence.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is not a dangerous infection or an autoimmune disease. It is a massive overgrowth of a normal yeast that lives on everyone's skin. The tropical heat caused it to overgrow, and the yeast produces a chemical that temporarily stops your skin from tanning, making the spots very visible.
Actionable Steps: Use the shampoo as a body wash exactly as directed. Understand that the white spots will still be there for the wedding, but they are no longer an active infection.

